payload.body:
@DasterProkio Thank you for the review. 

I have corrected the URL in the original description, to use the canonical path.

It is true that the accounting parts of Swarm rely on blockchain as their backend. But Swarm as a storage solution is a peer-to-peer decentralized storage solution - data is not stored on a blockchain. Therefore it fits into the Decentralized Systems category, adding the first such storage solution and making the list more complete.

@jordanricky1604-ship-it thank you for the review.

We are running the lint check locally, and there seems to be no errors found. I checked the headers and they do use the markdown headers and there also is the awesome badge next to title, already.

We did correct the capitalization errors (e.g. JavaScript), thank you.

@infonality thank you for the review. 

I re-checked the linter and there are no errors on my side. I was told that if copy-pasting the text, you indeed get some changes to headings etc., so maybe that was the case. 

As for being a blockchain project  - I have explained in https://github.com/sindresorhus/awesome/pull/4364#issuecomment-5236822313 what the connection is. It is using features of a blockchain to support part of the operation for the peer-to-peer storage, to make them possible.

@yboulaamane Thanks for actually opening the repos - both points addressed in [lesnik512/awesome-faststream@4440c9b](https://github.com/lesnik512/awesome-faststream/commit/4440c9b).

On the lint suppression: you were right that it shouldn't be there. Your suggested fix wouldn't have worked, though - linking the intro to the documentation site just moves the duplicate, since `https://faststream.ag2.ai` is already the Documentation entry in Official Resources. Instead I left "FastStream" unlinked in the intro paragraph; the logo above it still links to the site, and the repo link stays in Official Resources. `<!-- lint disable double-link -->` is gone and `awesome-lint` passes clean, so the rule is now active for the whole list.

On disclosure: I couldn't find a guideline requiring it, but it's a fair ask for a curated list, so I added a `Footnotes` section at the bottom, outside `Contents`. It names the six entries I maintain (the `modern-python` ones) and separately notes the two I only contribute to, so the distinction isn't blurred.

@atamano @dunemight thanks both, this was useful feedback.

@atamano I trimmed the most concentrated sections on main just now. I dropped the narrower sub-topic guides that a broader entry already covers (the separate ISA, IRP, and overseas-stocks guides all sit under the one personal-finance-accounts guide), removed a duplicative guide in Arrival and one in Healthcare where a directory already covers the same ground, and cut the audience list at the top that just repeated the intro. The sections you named are noticeably lighter now. Happy to trim further if a specific entry still looks weak.

@dunemight quick check that we are looking at the same line: the actual readme entry is "Settling in South Korea as a foreigner, covering visas, housing, healthcare, taxes, work, and culture.", which is the objective topic form. The "A curated list of..." wording you quoted is from the PR description above, not the entry itself. I kept the entry off the readme tagline on purpose, since that one repeats the list name. Glad to reword it if it still reads as list-describing to you.
